1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,000
Alright, so last topic, intertemporal consumption.

2
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:12,000
If you could get the door back there please.

3
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:19,000
Alright, so remember we've been doing general equilibrium theory.

4
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:26,000
And a lot of general equilibrium, in fact, if you're not paying close attention, you may think it's exactly the same as intermediate micro.

5
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:35,000
What general equilibrium adds though is the additional assumptions to understand what the whole economy does given every individual's optimization.

6
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:42,000
So in general equilibrium you do start with solving every individual's utility maximization problem, right?

7
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:46,000
Which you probably did in intermediate micro one years ago.

8
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:54,000
But then, given all of these optimization functions, you try to figure out what the equilibrium prices in the economy have to be.

9
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:59,000
And that's where general equilibrium theory adds something new to what you learned in intermediate micro.

10
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:06,000
Because you are actually deducing the behavior of the entire economy from the individuals in a way that is totally self-consistent if you've done it right.

11
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:08,000
Or almost totally self-consistent.

12
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:16,000
Remember there is, as long as we have a finite number of agents, there is this small inconsistency that everyone acts as if they have no effect on the price.

13
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:22,000
Or if you look really closely at the equations, you'll see that everybody does have a very small influence on the price.

14
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:28,000
So if you pay really close attention to the equation, we assume that everyone assumes that they can't affect the price at all.

15
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:31,000
Which is what we normally do when we are buying corn, right?

16
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:38,000
So if no one has ever bought a can of corn or a stalk of corn and then said, well I better not buy two, that might raise the world price of corn.

17
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:42,000
Is anyone worried that they are affecting world prices with their behavior?

18
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:48,000
So, that assumption is actually not so weird.

19
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:50,000
In fact, most of us use it most of the time.

20
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:57,000
But if you go and look at the actual equations that we are driving, they do apply that the price depends just a little bit on the behavior of the actors.

21
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:05,000
And the way to make it self-consistent is if you actually replace a finite number of agents with an infinite number of infinitesimal agents, and then it's all perfect.

22
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:13,000
Alright, so, and if you, this seems weird, you know, say, you know, Zeno's paradoxes and calculus, origins of calculus, remember anything about this?

23
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:17,000
Anyway, but for all practical purposes you don't need to worry about that.

24
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:27,000
Alright, and then we are applying, showing how general equilibrium theory has been used to understand markets where you have intertemporal markets and contingent claims markets.

25
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:33,000
Where this is a market where you are selling something where whether or not you have to deliver the good depends upon the conditions.

26
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:38,000
So you might sell someone oranges at the temperature in Florida is over 80 degrees.

27
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:45,000
And that is not a contract for oranges, it's a contract for contingent oranges, which only occurs under certain circumstances.

28
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:52,000
And that one of the great applications of those ideas is the idea of betting markets.

29
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:56,000
Which have actually been used in a number of real world circumstances.

30
00:02:56,000 --> 00:03:03,000
They've been used enough for us to know that they are better predictors of future outcomes than anything else that people have.

31
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:06,000
There is great prejudice against betting markets, of course.

32
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:08,000
Betting markets.

33
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:15,000
So at the time that Robin Hanson was involved in the so-called terrorism betting markets scandal,

34
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:22,000
one of the critics said, well clearly the way to be finding out whether there is going to be war between India and Pakistan is not by betting.

35
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:26,000
We need to go to the intelligence community and they'll tell us what's going to happen.

36
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:29,000
They could bet too if they really knew something.

37
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:33,000
But maybe they don't want to bet.

38
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:39,000
And there has been quite a bit of work on the psychology of what's going on in the intelligence community

39
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:47,000
where we see all kinds of extremely suspicious practices such as using words that are not falsifiable.

40
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:50,000
And why do you use non-falsifiable words?

41
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:56,000
So no one can ever prove that what you said was false.

42
00:03:56,000 --> 00:04:01,000
And including things like refusing to give actual probabilities.

43
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:04,000
Instead just say there is a distinct likelihood.

44
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:06,000
Which means what?

45
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:12,000
Well there is actually empirical literature on what a distinct likelihood means in terms of probability.

46
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:16,000
And it means anything between 10% and 90% of people.

47
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:21,000
It is a phrase that is so vague that it means almost anything.

48
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:24,000
Which makes it really easy to deny that you have ever been wrong.

49
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:29,000
Of course if you are making claims that are very different from everyone else,

50
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:32,000
and everyone else says they are being right and you have been proven wrong, that is kind of embarrassing.

51
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:37,000
So it is also a good idea to use the same kinds of vague adjectives that other people are using.

52
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:41,000
So if there is an error, it is like no one could have foreseen this.

53
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:45,000
How many people need to be in the betting market in order to get this?

54
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:46,000
So that is a great question.

55
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:49,000
So Robin would know, would have the best answer.

56
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:53,000
I know that experiments with 10 people, you are getting a lot of value out of that.

57
00:04:53,000 --> 00:05:02,000
And furthermore, even when you have betting markets where the stakes are capped at a fairly low level,

58
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:07,000
so like $100 a bet, those markets are not as good as ones where you can make millions,

59
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:12,000
but you get a lot of the value just out of having a little bit of money on the table.

60
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:17,000
And in fact if you know the work of Philip Ten Block who has a great book called Superforecasters,

61
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:23,000
you get a lot of value out of merely making people on the record say what is going to happen

62
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:28,000
with mathematical precision with no stakes at all other than their reputation.

63
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:33,000
So just making people say something specific and precise in advance,

64
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:39,000
before you have the information, and then seeing who was right and who was wrong and announcing it.

65
00:05:39,000 --> 00:05:43,000
That by itself actually gives us a lot, so betting markets add something on top of that.

66
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:50,000
But just to get people to the point where they specify what they mean is a tremendous achievement

67
00:05:50,000 --> 00:05:54,000
beyond what is normal in the real world.

68
00:05:54,000 --> 00:05:59,000
Like surely when wives are on the line, people would not just hide behind vague adjectives

69
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:03,000
in order to save their own skins.

70
00:06:03,000 --> 00:06:07,000
It's a pressing world, but it does happen.

71
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:10,000
Alright, now last application of general equilibrium theory we are going to do.

72
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:16,000
By the way, one that often pops up on exams and often times half the students have forgotten,

73
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:20,000
so don't be one of them, is for intertemporal consumption.

74
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:26,000
So you may remember back in macro, so let's see, who do we have? Carlos Ramirez?

75
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:30,000
Alright, so do you remember doing the small country model?

76
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:37,000
So the small country model is just the internationally denounced version of the individual who is so small

77
00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:40,000
that they don't have any effect on the world.

78
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:44,000
So just like you don't think that you're worried that you're going to change the world price of corn,

79
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:48,000
Luxembourg does not worry that they're going to change the world price of chocolate.

80
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:52,000
It's just one tiny country, they're not going to be changing anything.

81
00:06:52,000 --> 00:06:59,000
So anyway, it's very common for macroeconomists to go and apply general equilibrium theory ideas to countries

82
00:06:59,000 --> 00:07:04,000
and say let's assume that these are all small countries, no one country can change the world real interest rate

83
00:07:04,000 --> 00:07:10,000
or anything else, so then how do you move from analysis of individuals who are optimizing their consumption over time

84
00:07:10,000 --> 00:07:17,000
to small countries, or it could be individuals, small countries that optimize their consumption over time.

85
00:07:17,000 --> 00:07:24,000
So again, it's the same trick, so you want to find the prices that induce agri-consumption to equilibrate endowments.

86
00:07:24,000 --> 00:07:31,000
So you need to find the prices that convince people to not consume more than exists at any point in time.

87
00:07:31,000 --> 00:07:34,000
Because what is the most that we can consume in 2018?

88
00:07:34,000 --> 00:07:40,000
Or what is the most the world can consume in 2017?

89
00:07:40,000 --> 00:07:45,000
Well, we consume whatever we save from before, plus whatever we ever make this year.

90
00:07:45,000 --> 00:07:47,000
What about 2018?

91
00:07:47,000 --> 00:07:51,000
Yes, we can consume whatever we save from before, plus whatever we make in 2018, and so on.

92
00:07:51,000 --> 00:07:57,000
So it is not possible for us to eat our 2018 production this year.

93
00:07:57,000 --> 00:07:59,000
So that is not possible.

94
00:07:59,000 --> 00:08:05,000
So what you are going to be looking for is the prices that persuade people not to eat more than exists.

95
00:08:05,000 --> 00:08:11,000
Now is it possible for one individual to consume more than they have from last year, plus what they make this year?

96
00:08:11,000 --> 00:08:14,000
Of course. What is that called?

97
00:08:14,000 --> 00:08:16,000
Borrowing.

98
00:08:16,000 --> 00:08:22,000
Borrowing. So any one person can go and consume more than they have made this year, more than they had last year.

99
00:08:22,000 --> 00:08:24,000
All they have to do is borrow.

100
00:08:24,000 --> 00:08:32,000
So now of course in intertemporal markets we have a special word for the price that persuades people to not borrow too much.

101
00:08:32,000 --> 00:08:35,000
And that is the interest rate, of course.

102
00:08:35,000 --> 00:08:38,000
So this is a standard problem.

103
00:08:38,000 --> 00:08:41,000
So imagine we have a world populated by infinitely lived agents.

104
00:08:41,000 --> 00:08:46,000
So this way math works out more nicely than if people lived for 78 years.

105
00:08:46,000 --> 00:08:49,000
So infinity is easier to do the math in 78 years.

106
00:08:49,000 --> 00:08:53,000
It can be done for 78 years if you really want to.

107
00:08:53,000 --> 00:09:00,000
So one time a professor said he was going to go and figure out the effects of life extension on world interest rates.

108
00:09:00,000 --> 00:09:06,000
And I said, but don't we already have a model that assumes people are infinitely lived and are immortal?

109
00:09:06,000 --> 00:09:10,000
So we have already figured out what happens when we get maximum life extension.

110
00:09:10,000 --> 00:09:14,000
I mean really the innovation would be to do the opposite thing.

111
00:09:14,000 --> 00:09:16,000
Oh man.

112
00:09:16,000 --> 00:09:25,000
Anyway, so if every individual is going and maximizing the discounted sum of their utility.

113
00:09:25,000 --> 00:09:27,000
I'm going to assume log utility here.

114
00:09:27,000 --> 00:09:34,000
So log of consumption period T.

115
00:09:34,000 --> 00:09:40,000
So what this is saying is that in period 1 your utility is given by your log of consumption.

116
00:09:40,000 --> 00:09:46,000
In period 0 your consumption is given by log of consumption period 0.

117
00:09:46,000 --> 00:09:51,000
But then you're also maximizing that plus beta times the log of your consumption in period 1.

118
00:09:51,000 --> 00:09:55,000
Plus beta squared times the log of your consumption in period 2.

119
00:09:55,000 --> 00:09:57,000
And so on.

120
00:09:57,000 --> 00:10:00,000
So that beta measures your impatience.

121
00:10:00,000 --> 00:10:04,000
A high beta means that you are impatient or patient?

122
00:10:04,000 --> 00:10:07,000
Impatient.

123
00:10:07,000 --> 00:10:08,000
High beta, no.

124
00:10:08,000 --> 00:10:09,000
High beta means that you're very patient.

125
00:10:09,000 --> 00:10:14,000
Remember if beta is 1 then you care about the future just as much as you care about today.

126
00:10:14,000 --> 00:10:19,000
On the other hand if beta were 0 then let's see.

127
00:10:19,000 --> 00:10:22,000
So if 0 raised to the 0.

128
00:10:22,000 --> 00:10:27,000
I guess there's a division by 0 problem there.

129
00:10:27,000 --> 00:10:32,000
But if it were really close to 0 then you put almost all weight on your first period,

130
00:10:32,000 --> 00:10:35,000
on your consumption period 0 and then almost no weight on the future.

131
00:10:35,000 --> 00:10:41,000
So is that like 0.001? That raised to the 0 would be?

132
00:10:41,000 --> 00:10:45,000
What is 0 raised to the 0.001?

133
00:10:45,000 --> 00:10:49,000
0.001 raised to the 0.

134
00:10:49,000 --> 00:10:53,000
Is 0 raised to the 0? Is that anything? Is that defined?

135
00:10:53,000 --> 00:10:56,000
Is it undefined? I can't remember.

136
00:10:56,000 --> 00:11:00,000
I guess it would be 0 over 0.

137
00:11:00,000 --> 00:11:05,000
So if that were really close to 0 that would show that you're super impatient.

138
00:11:05,000 --> 00:11:11,000
That you do not care very much at all about anything other than the immediate situation.

139
00:11:11,000 --> 00:11:15,000
So if everybody has utility function like this,

140
00:11:15,000 --> 00:11:23,000
then if you just go and solve this using your intermediate micro or your intermediate micro slash macro,

141
00:11:23,000 --> 00:11:30,000
then you will find that in equilibrium every individual is going to set their consumption in period T plus 1 equal to beta

142
00:11:30,000 --> 00:11:35,000
times 1 plus r, the real interest rate, times their consumption in period T.

143
00:11:35,000 --> 00:11:38,000
So this is what you get for individual optimization.

144
00:11:38,000 --> 00:11:41,000
This is what any one utility maximizing person wants to do.

145
00:11:41,000 --> 00:11:48,000
And this is saying that if the interest rate is higher then you want to consume more in the future, less now.

146
00:11:48,000 --> 00:11:55,000
And if you are more impatient, or rather if you are more patient then you want to consume more in the future, less now.

147
00:11:55,000 --> 00:12:02,000
And in fact, if beta times 1 plus r equals 1 then you have a smooth consumption pattern.

148
00:12:02,000 --> 00:12:08,000
If this thing is actually less than 1 then you have declining consumption over time.

149
00:12:08,000 --> 00:12:15,000
And if the product of beta times 1 plus r is greater than 1 then you're going to have an increasing pattern of consumption over time.

150
00:12:15,000 --> 00:12:18,000
But now, what happens in general equilibrium?

151
00:12:18,000 --> 00:12:28,000
So in general equilibrium we have to figure out what value of r, the real interest rate, do we need in order to make the system stable?

152
00:12:28,000 --> 00:12:33,000
What interest rate will convince people to not consume more than exists?

153
00:12:33,000 --> 00:12:39,000
So let's suppose that endowments are constant and there's no way of saving consumption,

154
00:12:39,000 --> 00:12:43,000
so you have to consume this year's production in this period.

155
00:12:43,000 --> 00:12:47,000
And if everybody also has identical preferences then it is pretty clear.

156
00:12:47,000 --> 00:12:54,000
So if you need everyone to consume a constant amount over time then an equilibrium of that thing has to be 1, right?

157
00:12:54,000 --> 00:13:01,000
But remember, which of these two variables here is up to the individual?

158
00:13:01,000 --> 00:13:06,000
Yes, beta. So your impatience, that's just inside of you.

159
00:13:06,000 --> 00:13:11,000
How patient of a person am I? That's you, that's not society, at least in a simple model.

160
00:13:11,000 --> 00:13:14,000
So what has to change is r, the real interest rate has to change.

161
00:13:14,000 --> 00:13:16,000
And how does it have to change?

162
00:13:16,000 --> 00:13:21,000
Well, very simple, we need to have 1 plus r equal to 1 over beta.

163
00:13:21,000 --> 00:13:23,000
And does that intuitively make sense?

164
00:13:23,000 --> 00:13:28,000
Well, remember high beta means that people are going to be more patient.

165
00:13:28,000 --> 00:13:36,000
So and of course if something, so as beta gets, so beta is small meaning you're impatient,

166
00:13:36,000 --> 00:13:42,000
then 1 over that is, so if beta is small then 1 over beta is big,

167
00:13:42,000 --> 00:13:45,000
and therefore you're going to have a high real interest rate.

168
00:13:45,000 --> 00:13:49,000
On the other hand, if beta is close to 1 then you're going to have,

169
00:13:49,000 --> 00:13:54,000
this will also be close to 1 a little bit more, but you'll have a very low real interest rate.

170
00:13:54,000 --> 00:13:58,000
So which again makes a lot of intuitive sense.

171
00:13:58,000 --> 00:14:04,000
Alright, now you can imagine what if instead of having constant endowments we had growing endowments.

172
00:14:04,000 --> 00:14:07,000
So what if every period we had economic growth,

173
00:14:07,000 --> 00:14:11,000
so your consumption is going to be not whatever it was,

174
00:14:11,000 --> 00:14:16,000
or production will not be whatever it was last period, it will be 1 plus g times whatever it was.

175
00:14:16,000 --> 00:14:22,000
So imagine if every period consumption ct plus 1 is going to be equal to 1 plus g,

176
00:14:22,000 --> 00:14:26,000
the growth rate, times consumption period t.

177
00:14:26,000 --> 00:14:29,000
Alright, now what has to happen, well sort of first of all intuitively,

178
00:14:29,000 --> 00:14:32,000
so suppose you know you're getting an inheritance next year.

179
00:14:32,000 --> 00:14:35,000
What would you like to start doing today?

180
00:14:35,000 --> 00:14:38,000
Consuming more, right, borrowing against that future endowment.

181
00:14:38,000 --> 00:14:41,000
Now imagine the whole world knew it was getting rich next year.

182
00:14:41,000 --> 00:14:44,000
What would the whole world want to do?

183
00:14:44,000 --> 00:14:46,000
Yes, we all want to go and borrow.

184
00:14:46,000 --> 00:14:48,000
Can the whole world borrow?

185
00:14:48,000 --> 00:14:50,000
No, the whole world cannot borrow, right,

186
00:14:50,000 --> 00:14:53,000
because that production gain is happening in the future.

187
00:14:53,000 --> 00:14:56,000
We can't start eating our future production yet,

188
00:14:56,000 --> 00:15:01,000
so if people start expecting to get rich in the future, even though they're not rich today,

189
00:15:01,000 --> 00:15:04,000
then again, what could change?

190
00:15:04,000 --> 00:15:07,000
So that doesn't mean it's not going to be your patience changes,

191
00:15:07,000 --> 00:15:11,000
and again, in a simple story, it's not going to change the rate of growth that's just given.

192
00:15:11,000 --> 00:15:14,000
So then what is left to change?

193
00:15:14,000 --> 00:15:16,000
The interest rate.

194
00:15:16,000 --> 00:15:21,000
And if everybody wants to borrow, what has to happen to the interest rate to change their minds?

195
00:15:21,000 --> 00:15:23,000
It's got to go up, right?

196
00:15:23,000 --> 00:15:25,000
So this gives us the general equilibrium result,

197
00:15:25,000 --> 00:15:31,000
and the anticipation of economic growth raises real interest rates right now.

198
00:15:31,000 --> 00:15:35,000
So people can't borrow from one another if everyone's trying to consume more,

199
00:15:35,000 --> 00:15:38,000
but can't people consume more of their savings?

200
00:15:38,000 --> 00:15:41,000
Can people save less if they think that there's going to be a...

201
00:15:41,000 --> 00:15:46,000
Well, everybody can't save less because...

202
00:15:46,000 --> 00:15:51,000
If you have a potential consumption, then there's an actual consumption.

203
00:15:51,000 --> 00:15:53,000
The difference is your savings.

204
00:15:53,000 --> 00:15:57,000
So in this model, what do you do if you decide not to go and consume everything you have?

205
00:15:57,000 --> 00:15:58,000
What do you do?

206
00:15:58,000 --> 00:16:00,000
Lend it out to somebody else.

207
00:16:00,000 --> 00:16:05,000
And if you decide you're not going to lend it out to them, what does that person have to do?

208
00:16:05,000 --> 00:16:10,000
They've got to borrow it from somebody else or cut back on their own consumption.

209
00:16:10,000 --> 00:16:15,000
So yeah, I mean, again, the basic point that any one individual can change what they do is totally true,

210
00:16:15,000 --> 00:16:18,000
but again, it's not possible.

211
00:16:18,000 --> 00:16:21,000
In this model, it doesn't make sense for everyone to be saving

212
00:16:21,000 --> 00:16:24,000
because everybody wanted to save, what would happen to interest rates?

213
00:16:24,000 --> 00:16:26,000
They would go down, and then people would say,

214
00:16:26,000 --> 00:16:28,000
you know, with those lower interest rates, I don't feel like saving anymore.

215
00:16:28,000 --> 00:16:32,000
I feel like borrowing. Let's have a party.

216
00:16:32,000 --> 00:16:33,000
So that is the idea.

217
00:16:33,000 --> 00:16:37,000
So expected growth raises interest rates today,

218
00:16:37,000 --> 00:16:43,000
